1
00:00:03,166 --> 00:00:08,584
A plant hormone is made in small amounts in one part of the plant and acts in another.

2
00:00:08,584 --> 00:00:10,249
There are five classical ones.

3
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:14,358
Auxin's discovery is a favourite question.

4
00:00:14,358 --> 00:00:22,797
Darwin, in eighteen eighty: cover a coleoptile's tip and it no longer bends to light; cover the part below and it still bends.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,960
So the tip senses light but bending happens lower down.

6
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:33,425
Boysen-Jensen put a cut tip back on gelatin and it still bent: the signal is a diffusible chemical.

7
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:36,564
Paál got bending in darkness from an off-centre tip.

8
00:00:36,564 --> 00:00:43,843
Went collected the chemical in agar blocks; a block on one side made a cut coleoptile bend away.

9
00:00:43,843 --> 00:00:44,698
He named it auxin.

10
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,238
Auxin, made at the shoot tip from tryptophan, moves down.

11
00:00:50,238 --> 00:00:53,106
Light from one side drives it to the shaded side.

12
00:00:53,333 --> 00:01:01,235
The shaded side elongates more, so the shoot bends towards light: positive phototropism, the Cholodny Went hypothesis.

13
00:01:03,966 --> 00:01:09,020
Auxin from the apex also suppresses the buds below: apical dominance.

14
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:13,599
Pluck a tea bush tip and side buds grow, making the bush dense.

15
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:22,838
Synthetic auxins I B A and N A A root cuttings, and two four D kills broad-leaved weeds in rice and wheat.

16
00:01:22,838 --> 00:01:26,584
Careful: a dose that speeds a stem stops a root.

17
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:36,782
Gibberellin came from bakanae, the foolish seedling disease of rice, where infected seedlings grow tall and pale.

18
00:01:36,782 --> 00:01:40,623
Kurosawa showed a cell-free fungal extract did the same.

19
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:45,350
Its signature effect is stem elongation.

20
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:50,833
A rosette cabbage given gibberellin shoots up a stem and flowers: bolting.

21
00:01:51,033 --> 00:02:00,247
It also makes dwarf peas tall, breaks dormancy, triggers alpha-amylase in germinating barley, and enlarges Thompson Seedless grapes.

22
00:02:01,866 --> 00:02:05,800
Cytokinins promote cell division, with auxin present.

23
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,214
Kinetin came from herring sperm DNA, zeatin from maize.

24
00:02:10,214 --> 00:02:11,534
They are made in root tips.

25
00:02:13,166 --> 00:02:21,410
In tissue culture the ratio decides: more auxin gives roots, equal gives callus, more cytokinin gives shoots.

26
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:24,241
This is the fact most often reversed.

27
00:02:25,466 --> 00:02:32,499
Cytokinin delays ageing: a treated spot on a detached leaf stays green while the rest yellows.

28
00:02:32,499 --> 00:02:33,999
That is the Richmond Lang effect.

29
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,013
Pause and think.

30
00:02:38,013 --> 00:02:40,526
Plenty of cytokinin, little auxin.

31
00:02:40,526 --> 00:02:42,022
What grows from the callus?

32
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:49,813
Shoots.

33
00:02:49,813 --> 00:02:55,021
High cytokinin gives shoots, high auxin roots, equal amounts callus.

34
00:02:55,021 --> 00:03:00,118
Roots is the trap: the exact reversal, and the commonest mistake here.

35
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:04,912
Abscisic acid, A B A, is the stress hormone.

36
00:03:04,912 --> 00:03:07,687
In drought it closes stomata within minutes.

37
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:12,590
It also induces seed and bud dormancy, opposing gibberellin.

38
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:19,449
Trap: despite its name, A B A is not the main leaf-fall hormone.

39
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:22,324
That is ethylene; auxin delays abscission.

40
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:28,131
Ethylene is the only gaseous hormone, made from methionine.

41
00:03:28,131 --> 00:03:30,431
Its signature job: ripening fruit.

42
00:03:30,633 --> 00:03:39,588
Climacteric fruits like banana and mango release an autocatalytic burst of ethylene, so one ripe banana ripens its neighbours.

43
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:45,632
Orange, grape and litchi are non-climacteric and won't ripen after picking.

44
00:03:45,632 --> 00:03:51,477
Ethylene also causes leaf fall and epinasty, and is sprayed as ethephon.

45
00:03:53,100 --> 00:04:00,003
Warning: calcium carbide releases acetylene, not ethylene, plus toxic impurities.

46
00:04:00,003 --> 00:04:01,339
It is prohibited in Nepal.

47
00:04:02,566 --> 00:04:15,937
All five: auxin for elongation and apical dominance; gibberellin for bolting; cytokinin for division; A B A for stomatal closure and dormancy; ethylene for ripening and leaf fall.

48
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:24,992
Growth is an irreversible increase in size or dry weight.

49
00:04:24,992 --> 00:04:28,313
A wilted leaf that recovers has not grown.

50
00:04:28,313 --> 00:04:32,238
Plant growth is localised in meristems and indeterminate.

51
00:04:33,466 --> 00:04:38,739
In a root tip, cells divide behind the cap, then elongate, then mature.

52
00:04:38,966 --> 00:04:44,223
Ink marks spread most in the elongation zone: that is where the root lengthens.

53
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:50,877
Plot size against time and you get an S-shaped curve: the grand period of growth.

54
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:59,324
Four phases: slow lag, exponential log, deceleration as resources run short, and stationary at maturity.

55
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:04,112
The steepest point, the point of inflexion, is the fastest growth.

56
00:05:04,333 --> 00:05:09,673
Early on both daughter cells divide: geometric, exponential growth.

57
00:05:09,673 --> 00:05:13,371
Later only one does: arithmetic growth.

58
00:05:13,371 --> 00:05:15,165
Then it stops, giving the S.

59
00:05:16,366 --> 00:05:24,645
Many plants time flowering by day and night length: photoperiodism, discovered by Garner and Allard in nineteen twenty.

60
00:05:24,866 --> 00:05:35,506
Short-day plants, like chrysanthemum and soybean, need nights longer than a critical length; long-day plants, like wheat and radish, need shorter ones.

61
00:05:35,506 --> 00:05:37,602
Here, say twelve hours.

62
00:05:37,602 --> 00:05:39,215
Tomato is day-neutral.

63
00:05:39,433 --> 00:05:47,234
Break a long night with a flash of light: the short-day plant stops flowering, the long-day plant flowers.

64
00:05:47,234 --> 00:05:49,973
So plants measure unbroken darkness.

65
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:55,842
The leaves sense it with phytochrome, and a signal, florigen, travels to the shoot tip.

66
00:05:57,066 --> 00:06:03,467
Tropic movements are growth movements whose direction follows the stimulus, like phototropism.

67
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:06,538
Lay a seedling sideways.

68
00:06:06,538 --> 00:06:12,083
Statoliths settle in the root cap, and auxin collects on the lower side of root and shoot.

69
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:16,065
In the shoot that speeds lower-side growth, so it curves up.

70
00:06:16,065 --> 00:06:21,658
Roots are more sensitive, so the same auxin inhibits them, and the root curves down.

71
00:06:24,366 --> 00:06:29,472
Nastic movements have a direction fixed by the organ, not the stimulus.

72
00:06:29,472 --> 00:06:34,966
Some are growth movements, like Crocus flowers opening; others are turgor movements.

73
00:06:35,166 --> 00:06:38,097
Touch Mimosa pudica, lajjawati.

74
00:06:38,097 --> 00:06:43,524
An electrical signal spreads, the leaflets fold in pairs, and the leaf droops.

75
00:06:43,733 --> 00:06:51,594
In the pulvinus at the leaf base, lower motor cells lose potassium and water and go limp, so the leaf drops.

76
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:57,688
Nothing grows; within ten to thirty minutes the cells refill and the leaf rises.

77
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:05,438
So: tropic movements follow the stimulus, are always growth, and are permanent.

78
00:07:05,438 --> 00:07:11,567
Nastic movements follow the organ, may be growth or turgor, and turgor ones reverse.

79
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:16,926
Last one.

80
00:07:16,926 --> 00:07:19,934
A lajjawati leaf folds when touched.

81
00:07:19,934 --> 00:07:20,921
Which movement is it?

82
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,122
Seismonasty: a nastic turgor movement.

83
00:07:31,122 --> 00:07:36,764
Thigmotropism is the trap: it is permanent directional growth, like a tendril coiling.

84
00:07:38,133 --> 00:07:39,593
Exam tip.

85
00:07:39,593 --> 00:07:45,088
Mimosa: seismonasty, nastic, turgor, reversible.

86
00:07:45,088 --> 00:07:49,377
Tendril: thigmotropism, tropic, growth, permanent.

87
00:07:51,566 --> 00:07:52,635
Quick recap.

88
00:07:52,635 --> 00:07:58,469
Water moves to more negative potential, and xylem sap is pulled under tension.

89
00:07:58,469 --> 00:08:02,440
Potassium opens stomata; A B A closes them.

90
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:07,974
The light reaction makes ATP, NADPH and oxygen from water.

91
00:08:07,974 --> 00:08:16,893
Calvin needs three ATP and two NADPH per carbon dioxide; C4 separates steps in space, CAM in time.

92
00:08:16,893 --> 00:08:23,391
Respiration gives thirty to thirty-two ATP; R Q is about zero point seven for fat.

93
00:08:23,391 --> 00:08:28,207
And five hormones; tropisms are growth, Mimosa is turgor.

94
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,094
That was plant physiology.

95
00:08:34,094 --> 00:08:38,105
Remember the sal: the sun pulls a rope of water through it.

96
00:08:38,105 --> 00:08:41,139
Next, Unit 3, genetics.

97
00:08:41,139 --> 00:08:41,718
See you there.

